{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghTzNKkk1X2rtgiuTqhP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dq = 1070 cm⁻¹ (10Dq = 10 700 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 862 cm⁻¹.** The bands fit a high-spin octahedral d⁷ ion whose ground term is ⁴T₁g(F).\n\n**Model**\n\nIn the Tanabe–Sugano model for high-spin d⁷, measured relative to the barycentre:\n- ⁴T₂g = +2Dq\n- ⁴A₂g = +12Dq\n- The two ⁴T₁g states mix. Their unmixed energies are –6Dq for ⁴T₁g(F) and 15B for ⁴T₁g(P), and the coupling between them is 4Dq.\n\nSolving the 2×2 mixing problem gives the transition energies, where S = √(225B² + 180B·Dq + 100Dq²):\n- ν₁ (⁴T₁g → ⁴T₂g) = 5Dq – 7.5B + ½S\n- ν₂ (⁴T₁g → ⁴A₂g) = 15Dq – 7.5B + ½S\n- ν₃ (⁴T₁g → ⁴T₁g(P)) = S\n\nThis means ν₂ – ν₁ = 10Dq exactly.\n\n**Assigning the bands**\n\n- **Chosen assignment:** ν₁ = 9 460, ν₂ = 20 160, ν₃ = 21 160 cm⁻¹.\n  - 10Dq = 20 160 – 9 460 = 10 700, so Dq = 1 070 cm⁻¹.\n  - Putting this into ν₁: 9 460 = 5 350 – 7.5B + 10 580, so 7.5B = 6 470 and B ≈ 863 cm⁻¹.\n  - Check: these values predict ν₃ = S ≈ 21 168 cm⁻¹, against 21 160 observed. That agrees within about 8 cm⁻¹.\n  - Fitting B to ν₃ instead, with Dq = 1 070, gives B ≈ 862 cm⁻¹, which is consistent.\n- **Swapped assignment ruled out:** taking ν₂ = 21 160 and ν₃ = 20 160 gives Dq = 1 170 and B ≈ 863. But those values predict ν₃ ≈ 22 045 cm⁻¹, which does not match.\n\n**Why C cannot be found**\n\nThe Racah parameter C cannot be determined from these data. All three bands are quartet-to-quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B. C would need spin-forbidden bands (to doublet states), and none are given.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1070, \"B\": 862, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"The bands fit high-spin d7 (nu2-nu1=10Dq, nu3 checks), but C needs spin-forbidden bands, which are not given.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_inpu